CORE
main.cpp
--cpp11 --pointer-check
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ returned through a variadic call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ returned through a variadic template call: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ no variadic arguments: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.4\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^\*\* 1 of \d+ failed
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
dropped the body
--
N5008 [class.copy.elis], [stmt.return]/2: a by-value return of a class that
is not trivially copyable is lowered (elide_cpp_returned_temporaries) to
construction in the caller's storage through a hidden result parameter,
appended after the declared parameters.  For a variadic function
([dcl.fct]/6: a parameter-declaration-clause ending in an ellipsis, also
`F first...' with a non-pack F -- libc++'s `__bind_back(_Fn...)') the call
site appended the result address after ALL arguments, so it was bound to
the first variadic argument's position and the first variadic argument
became the result pointer: the callee wrote through `3' ("pointer invalid
in *#result").  The address now precedes the variadic arguments.  Fixed in
round 163b (surfaced by cpp14_perfect_forward_struct_arity once
value-initialization writes the zeroed object through the result pointer).
